Bacterial Enteritis (Salmonella typhimurium)
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Gastrointestinal Loss
Acute non-bloody watery diarrhea, Fever and chills, Diffuse abdominal cramping

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-008
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe vomiting and voluminous diarrhea resulting in profound weakness and inability to maintain oral hydration, requiring urgent after-hours evaluation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage highlighted a rapid onset of high fever, recurrent vomiting, and voluminous watery diarrhea leading to orthostatic dizziness. This clinical presentation demanded an urgent, in-person assessment to evaluate intravascular volume status and provide parenteral antiemetics to facilitate oral rehydration prior to the next morning's in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Severe watery diarrhea, vomiting, and high fever. Patient states, I can't keep a single drop of water down, my stomach is in knots, and I feel incredibly weak.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced abruptly 12 hours ago with an acute onset of non-bloody, voluminous watery diarrhea. This was rapidly followed by diffuse abdominal cramping, nausea, and recurrent vomiting, preventing any meaningful fluid intake. The patient reports a documented fever peaking at 38.8C, accompanied by severe chills and rigors. Profound fatigue, malaise, and muscle weakness are prominent. The patient experienced a near-syncopal episode upon standing to walk to the bathroom, indicating evolving orthostatic hypotension and significant volume depletion.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to profound lethargy and orthostatic dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of hypovolemic collapse if fluid losses continue unabated; roommate is present to assist.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 mmHg | HR: 118 bpm | Temp: 38.7 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 72.0 Kg
General: Appears acutely unwell, flushed, diaphoretic, and lethargic.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Significant tachycardia. Weak radial pulses. Pronounced orthostatic hypotension is present on assessment. Respiratory: Clear bilaterally. Gastrointestinal: Abdomen is soft but exhibits diffuse tenderness to palpation, accompanied by hyperactive, loud bowel sounds. No guarding, rigidity, or focal tenderness. Integumentary: The diagnostic triad is clinically confirmed: acute non-bloody watery diarrhea, fever and chills, and diffuse abdominal cramping. Examination reveals dry mucous membranes and delayed capillary refill (3 seconds), confirming moderate to severe dehydration secondary to gastrointestinal loss.
Environmental Observations: Bedroom is close to the bathroom, minimizing transit distance.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient takes no daily medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Bacterial Enteritis (Salmonella typhimurium) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Viral Gastroenteritis (Norovirus) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a high fever exceeding 38.5C and severe systemic toxicity (chills, rigors), which are less typical of uncomplicated viral enteritis.

Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sudden hyper-acute onset, lack of blood in the stool, and absence of a chronic history of altered bowel habits.

Food Poisoning (Staphylococcus aureus toxin) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Prominent high fever and systemic signs, whereas preformed toxin ingestion typically presents with profound vomiting and afebrile presentation within hours of consumption.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of abrupt, voluminous watery diarrhea, recurrent vomiting, high fever, and rigors is highly consistent with an acute systemic bacterial enteritis, likely Salmonella. The inability to tolerate oral fluids due to intractable vomiting has rapidly precipitated moderate hypovolemia, evidenced by tachycardia, dry mucous membranes, and overt orthostatic hypotension. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have inevitably resulted in severe dehydration requiring emergency hospital admission for intravenous fluid resuscitation. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively assess perfusion, rule out an acute surgical abdomen, and administer a parenteral antiemetic to halt the vomiting cycle and safely initiate aggressive oral rehydration therapy in the home setting. The expected investigation results include isolation of non-lactose fermenting, hydrogen sulfide-producing Gram-negative bacilli on standard stool culture that would assist in confirming the diagnosis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ondansetron 4mg sublingually to rapidly control nausea and vomiting. Administered Paracetamol 1000mg PO for fever and abdominal discomfort once nausea subsided. Initiated strict oral rehydration therapy.
Positive Behavioral Change: To rebuild your fluid levels without triggering vomiting, commit to taking one small sip (15ml) of an oral rehydration solution every 10 minutes. Use a timer on your phone. When the timer goes off, take the sip, and verbally acknowledge that the fluid stayed down. The steady reduction in your dizziness and nausea will reward your patience and compliance with this slow, systematic hydration process.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 24-48 hours for stool culture if symptoms do not improve, or immediately if deterioration occurs.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the nearest Emergency Department or call 000 if you cannot keep any fluids down for the next 4 hours despite the anti-nausea medication, if you develop severe, constant abdominal pain, or if you become confused or completely unable to stand.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive based on typical clinical features of foodborne bacterial enteritis.
Clinical Course: The illness is generally self-limiting over 4-7 days. The critical intervention is maintaining hydration to support the body while it clears the infection naturally; antibiotics are rarely indicated unless systemic complications arise.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to drink large volumes of fluid at once, as this will acutely stretch your irritated stomach and cause immediate vomiting, worsening your dehydration.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The sublingual wafer bypasses your stomach to turn off the nausea center in your brain. This allows us a window to slowly trickle electrolytes back into your system to correct your low blood pressure.
Actionable Steps: Take one sip of rehydration solution every 10 minutes using a timer. Avoid solid foods until fluids are completely tolerated.

